\begin{problem}
Here is a Turing machine program using the syntax of the simulator we used in class.
The start state is 0 and the input alphabet is $\{a, b\}$.

\begin{verbatim}
0 a b R 1
0 b a R 2

1 a a L 0
1 b b L 3

2 a b R 3
2 b a R 0

3 a a L 2
3 b b L 1
\end{verbatim}


\noindent
Suppose we run this machine with input
\ifversionA $baaba$ \else $aabaa$\fi.
Simulate the Turing machine \textbf{for 5 steps or until it halts}, whichever comes first.
\begin{enumerate}
\item
Write down the state the machine is in \emph{after each step.}
\item
Write down the the tape contents \emph{at the end of your simulation.}
\item
Use an arrow to indicate the location of the read/write head
at the end of your simulation.
\end{enumerate}

\def\blank{\rule{.5in}{.5pt}}

\begin{center}
\bigskip
\Large
States: 0 \ \ \blank \ \ \blank \ \ \blank \ \ \blank \ \ \blank

\bigskip
\bigskip

Tape Contents (at end of simulation, arrow pointing to read/write head position):

\medskip
\medskip

$\cdots$
\begin{tabular}{c|c|c|c|c|c|c|c|c|c|c|c}
\hline
\phantom{X} &
\phantom{X} &
\phantom{X} &
\phantom{X} &
\phantom{X} &
\phantom{X} &
\phantom{X} &
\phantom{X} &
\phantom{X} &
\phantom{X} &
\phantom{X} &
\phantom{X}
\\
\hline
\end{tabular}
$\cdots$
\end{center}

\end{problem}

\begin{problem}
Explain how we know that $\{0^n1^n \mid n = 0, 1, 2, \dots \}$ is not
a regular language.
\end{problem}

